proxmod-cron (202608.22.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Frontend: the asset no longer runs in strict mode. ExtJS resolves
    callParent by reading Function.caller on the calling method, and V8 hands
    out null for that whenever the caller is a strict-mode function, so every
    callParent in this file failed inside ext-all.js with "Cannot read
    properties of null (reading 'apply')" and the panel it belonged to never
    built. Strictness is inherited by nested functions, so the directive is
    gone from the file rather than from one method. Needs proxmod 0.2.1, which
    carried the same defect in its own asset.
  * Logging never blocks a job. A journald datagram socket nobody is draining
    fills up and send() on it blocks with no timeout — every container, where a
    fresh network namespace caps net.unix.max_dgram_qlen at ten. A job wedged
    inside a log write holds its run lock forever, which is far worse than a
    lost log line, so the send is bounded by SO_SNDTIMEO and a socket that
    times out is abandoned for the rest of the process in favour of the
    existing logger/stderr ladder.

 -- proxmod maintainers <proxmod@localhost>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000

proxmod-cron (202608.17.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial release: cron management for Proxmox VE as a proxmod extension.
  * Cluster and node definition stores, rendered into /etc/cron.d by a
    one-line anchor conffile that needs no daemon and no maintainer script.
  * Job-type plugin registry, Perl client and JavaScript namespace for other
    extensions; a job an extension created can be enabled and disabled by an
    administrator but not edited or removed behind its owner's back.
  * Run tracking and captured output as structured journald entries, read back
    with journalctl field matches; the last-run file under /var/lib is only a
    cache and is rebuildable with `proxmod-cronctl reindex`.
  * Cluster job placement: `run_on: any` runs a job on exactly one node per
    scheduled minute, chosen by racing for an atomic lease in /etc/pve, so the
    job moves to a surviving node with no leader, heartbeat or election. A node
    that is not quorate stands down from every cluster-scoped job and records a
    skip; node-scoped jobs and standalone hosts are unaffected.
  * Journal writes are bounded: a journald that has stopped draining its socket
    can no longer block a running job inside a log write, which would have held
    the job's run lock indefinitely. The entry falls to logger and then to
    stderr, as it already did for a missing socket.
  * Access control expressed entirely in existing PVE privileges on existing
    ACL paths, with per-job delegation for job types that declare what object
    they are about.

 -- proxmod-cron maintainers <proxmod-cron@localhost>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
